e use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx+2=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x+3}).\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx+2=(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x+2 & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-2 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)) & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-2}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290539 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290539 #edb/ref 1069 "q-242914" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290539 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290539 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${3}^{4-x}={8}^{2x+3}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290539 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d1a98c1d-e002-56dc-98cf-733b76c221c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290539 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{4-x} & ={8}^{2x+3} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{4-x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({8}^{2x+3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n(4-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)=(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ -x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)) & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ x & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290543 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290543 #edb/ref 1069 "q-242923" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290543 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290543 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the solution of ${3}^{2x}={8}^{1-6x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290543 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "399f64e6-0f82-5ca3-861d-fbe99de11ba6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290543 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2x} & ={8}^{1-6x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{2x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({8}^{1-6x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =(1-6x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =(1-6x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)-6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ 2x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8))}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333008 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡3}{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333008 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333008 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "023d00f0-da47-5394-ae60-c245b8db80c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333093 #edb/ref 1080 "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡4" #edb