 [17592186566976 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566976 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566976 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186566811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566976 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "db5c3cce-a76e-57c9-807c-d1d10889bec0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566980 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566980 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566980 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566980 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566980 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566980 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566980 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566980 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186566603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566980 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "df525b75-5def-5db5-bf1c-dec1da3b1a21" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567038 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567038 #edb/ref 1069 "q-100469" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567038 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567038 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the solution of ${3}^{x+4}={6}^{2-3x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567038 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "532b62bc-05d4-54bd-b88c-d3ad9a2bd774" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567038 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x+4} & ={6}^{2-3x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{x+4}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{2-3x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567094 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567094 #edb/ref 1069 "q-233241" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567094 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567094 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${3}^{x-2}={5}^{4x},$ what is the value of $x?$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567094 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e11adf3d-e629-5f12-b77c-eadc477f80fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567094 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we take ${log}_{3}$ on both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x-2} & ={5}^{4x} \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{x-2}) & ={log}_{3}⁡({5}^{4x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nx-2={log}_{3}⁡({5}^{4x}).\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx-2=4x{log}_{3}⁡(5)\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for $x,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =4x{log}_{3}⁡(5) \\\\ x & =2+4x{log}_{3}⁡(5) \\\\ x-4x{log}_{3}⁡(5) & =2 \\\\ x(1-4{log}_{3}⁡(5)) & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{1-4{log}_{3}⁡(5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the change of base formula, we have\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡(5)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}≈1.464\\,974.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $x$ as\n\n$$\nx≈\\frac{2}{1-4(1.464\\,974)}≈-0.412,\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567103 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567103 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98908" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567103 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567103 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${3}^{x}={2}^{x-1},$ what is the value of $x?$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567103 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f054daca-f42a-55cb-aa6e-67968f5ef4ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567103 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we take ${log}_{3}$ on both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x} & ={2}^{x-1} \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{x}) & ={log}_{3}⁡({2}^{x-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{3}⁡({2}^{x-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(x-1){log}_{3}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(x-1){log}_{3}⁡(2) \\\\ x & =x{log}_{3}⁡(2)-{log}_{3}⁡(2) \\\\ x-x{log}_{3}⁡(2) & =-{log}_{3}⁡(2) \\\\ x(1-{log}_{3}⁡(2)) & =-{log}_{3}⁡(2) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(2)}{1-{log}_{3}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the change of base formula, we have\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡(2)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}≈0.630\\,930.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $x$ as\n\n$$\nx≈-\\frac{0.630\\,930}{1-0.630\\,930}≈-1.710,\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
